WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.433 --> 00:00:08.333
The first film built a test on paper: can a species grow beside its competitor?

2
00:00:09.753 --> 00:00:14.133
On a rocky Scottish shore, what keeps one barnacle out of the band just below it?

3
00:00:15.117 --> 00:00:20.377
At Millport, adults of the barnacle Chthamalus, in teal, live high on the rock.

4
00:00:21.677 --> 00:00:25.197
Another barnacle, Balanus, in orange, covers the rock below.

5
00:00:26.537 --> 00:00:29.057
A pattern like that has rival explanations.

6
00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:35.380
Lower down, Chthamalus might not tolerate the longer time underwater.

7
00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:39.800
Its predators might be worse there, such as the whelk Thais.

8
00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:42.880
Or Balanus might be crowding it out.

9
00:00:44.533 --> 00:00:48.653
Joseph Connell tested the crowding explanation by changing it.

10
00:00:49.773 --> 00:00:53.413
He mapped patches of Chthamalus and split each map in two.

11
00:00:54.753 --> 00:00:56.673
A coin toss chose one half for removal.

12
00:00:57.783 --> 00:01:03.143
On that half, he removed every Balanus touching or surrounding a Chthamalus.

13
00:01:04.423 --> 00:01:09.283
The other half stayed untouched, with its Balanus in place, at the same height and

14
00:01:09.283 --> 00:01:10.283
in the same weather.

15
00:01:11.083 --> 00:01:15.723
Then he came back at intervals and recounted the marked barnacles on both halves.

16
00:01:17.003 --> 00:01:21.163
The difference he intended between the halves was whether Balanus was there.

17
00:01:22.217 --> 00:01:27.837
Suppose longer time underwater was what killed Chthamalus low on the shore, and

18
00:01:27.837 --> 00:01:28.877
Balanus did nothing.

19
00:01:30.237 --> 00:01:33.157
What would the cleared half show, compared with the untouched half?

20
00:01:39.683 --> 00:01:44.383
If immersion were the cause, both halves would do the same, since both spend the

21
00:01:44.383 --> 00:01:45.463
same time underwater.

22
00:01:46.843 --> 00:01:48.363
That is not what Connell found.

23
00:01:49.843 --> 00:01:54.343
Chthamalus kept free of Balanus survived well, at the shore levels he studied.

24
00:01:55.467 --> 00:01:57.947
He also watched how Chthamalus died.

25
00:01:59.567 --> 00:02:02.427
Growing Balanus smothered it, undercut it and crushed it.

26
00:02:03.487 --> 00:02:08.987
That is interference competition, one barnacle acting directly on another, much of

27
00:02:08.987 --> 00:02:10.127
it for space on the rock.

28
00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:15.190
So at Millport, Balanus set the lower limit of Chthamalus.

29
00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:21.090
The band where Chthamalus could live reaches lower than the band where it does live.

30
00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:25.030
Those are its fundamental and realized niches.

31
00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:32.300
Keep the scope. This is one shore, one pair of species and one set of years.

32
00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:36.720
It does not say what sets barnacle limits on every coast.

33
00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:40.720
Its strength is the design, not the generality.

34
00:02:42.283 --> 00:02:46.943
For a neighbour that stays put, like a barnacle, the design gives a field test.

35
00:02:48.243 --> 00:02:51.243
Remove it from a random half, and leave a matched half alone.

36
00:02:52.543 --> 00:02:57.703
If survival improves, and proper controls rule out other differences, the neighbour

37
00:02:57.703 --> 00:02:58.303
was limiting.
